export const CS_NOTES = {
  "Structure and Function of the Processor": "<h3>Von Neumann architecture</h3>\n      <p>The stored program concept: both instructions and data are held in the same main memory (RAM) and are fetched, decoded and executed sequentially by the CPU.</p>\n      <h3>Key CPU registers</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>PC (Program Counter):</strong> holds the address of the NEXT instruction to be fetched</li>\n        <li><strong>MAR (Memory Address Register):</strong> holds the address of the memory location currently being accessed</li>\n        <li><strong>MDR (Memory Data Register):</strong> holds the data being transferred to/from memory</li>\n        <li><strong>CIR (Current Instruction Register):</strong> holds the instruction currently being decoded/executed</li>\n        <li><strong>ACC (Accumulator):</strong> holds the results of calculations performed by the ALU</li>\n        <li><strong>Status/Flag register:</strong> holds flags indicating the outcome of the last ALU operation (e.g. zero, carry, overflow, negative)</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Buses</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Address bus:</strong> unidirectional; carries the memory address to be read/written</li>\n        <li><strong>Data bus:</strong> bidirectional; carries the actual data</li>\n        <li><strong>Control bus:</strong> carries control signals (e.g. read/write, clock signals)</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>The Fetch-Decode-Execute (FDE) cycle</h3>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Fetch:</strong> the address in the PC is copied to the MAR; the instruction at that address is copied to the MDR then into the CIR; the PC is incremented</li>\n        <li><strong>Decode:</strong> the Control Unit decodes the instruction in the CIR to determine what operation is required</li>\n        <li><strong>Execute:</strong> the instruction is carried out (e.g. an ALU calculation, a memory read/write, or a jump)</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h3>The ALU and Control Unit</h3>\n      <p>The <strong>ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit)</strong> performs arithmetic (add, subtract) and logical (AND, OR, comparison) operations. The <strong>Control Unit</strong> coordinates and manages the whole FDE cycle, decoding instructions and generating the control signals to execute them.</p>\n      <h3>Cache and performance</h3>\n      <p>Cache is small, very fast memory located close to the CPU that stores frequently/recently used data and instructions, reducing the need to access much slower RAM. Factors affecting CPU performance: clock speed (GHz), number of cores, cache size, and word/bus width.</p>\n      <p class='exam-tip'><strong>Exam tip:</strong> for \"describe the fetch stage\" questions, name the specific registers (PC→MAR, MDR→CIR) — vague answers lose marks.</p>\n      <h3>Worked example: tracing a simple FDE cycle</h3>\n      <p>Suppose the PC contains address 100, and the instruction stored at address 100 is \"LOAD 200\" (load the value at address 200 into the accumulator):</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Fetch: 100 is copied from PC to MAR → the instruction \"LOAD 200\" at address 100 is copied to MDR, then to CIR → PC is incremented to 101</li>\n        <li>Decode: the Control Unit interprets \"LOAD 200\" as an instruction to fetch the value at address 200 into the ACC</li>\n        <li>Execute: 200 is placed in the MAR, the value at that address is copied via MDR into the ACC</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h3>Clock speed and the system clock</h3>\n      <p>The system clock generates regular electrical pulses that synchronise the timing of operations within the CPU. Clock speed (measured in Hz, typically GHz for modern CPUs) is the number of these pulses per second. A higher clock speed generally means more FDE cycles can be completed per second, but this is only one of several factors affecting overall performance (cache size, core count, and instruction set efficiency all matter too).</p>",
  "Types of Processor": "<h3>CISC vs RISC</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>CISC (Complex Instruction Set Computer):</strong> large set of complex, variable-length instructions; fewer registers; instructions can take multiple clock cycles; relies more on microcode (e.g. x86)</li>\n        <li><strong>RISC (Reduced Instruction Set Computer):</strong> small set of simple, fixed-length instructions that typically execute in one clock cycle; more registers; simpler hardware, enabling deeper pipelining (e.g. ARM)</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Multicore and multiprocessor systems</h3>\n      <p><strong>Multicore:</strong> multiple processing cores on a single chip, sharing some resources (like cache), able to execute instructions in parallel. <strong>Multiprocessor:</strong> multiple separate CPU chips within one system.</p>\n      <h3>Parallel processing and Flynn's taxonomy</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>SISD</strong> (Single Instruction, Single Data) — traditional sequential processor</li>\n        <li><strong>SIMD</strong> (Single Instruction, Multiple Data) — one instruction applied to many data items simultaneously (e.g. GPUs)</li>\n        <li><strong>MISD</strong> (Multiple Instruction, Single Data) — rare, used in fault-tolerant systems</li>\n        <li><strong>MIMD</strong> (Multiple Instruction, Multiple Data) — multiple independent processors working on different data (e.g. multicore systems)</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>GPUs</h3>\n      <p>GPUs (Graphics Processing Units) contain hundreds/thousands of simple cores optimised for performing the same simple operation on large amounts of data in parallel (SIMD) — ideal for graphics rendering and, increasingly, machine learning matrix calculations.</p>\n      <h3>Pipelining</h3>\n      <p>Overlapping the fetch, decode and execute stages of consecutive instructions so multiple instructions are processed simultaneously (at different stages), increasing instruction throughput. Limited by <strong>pipeline hazards</strong>: data hazards, control hazards (branches), and structural hazards (resource conflicts).</p>\n      <h3>Worked example: pipelining timeline</h3>\n      <p>Without pipelining, 3 instructions each taking 3 stages (F, D, E) would take 9 clock cycles total (3 each, done one at a time). With pipelining, while instruction 1 is being decoded, instruction 2 can be fetched; while instruction 1 executes, instruction 2 decodes and instruction 3 fetches. This overlap means the 3 instructions complete in only 5 cycles rather than 9 — a clear throughput gain that increases further with more instructions.</p>\n      <h3>Embedded processors</h3>\n      <p>A specialised, often lower-power processor designed for one specific purpose within a larger device (e.g. inside a washing machine, car engine, or games console controller), as opposed to a general-purpose processor found in a desktop PC that must handle a huge variety of unpredictable tasks.</p>",
  "Input, Output and Storage": "<h3>RAM and ROM</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>RAM (Random Access Memory):</strong> volatile — loses contents when power is removed. Used for programs/data currently in use</li>\n        <li><strong>ROM (Read Only Memory):</strong> non-volatile — retains contents without power. Used to store the bootstrap/BIOS needed to start the computer</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Secondary storage technologies</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Magnetic (HDD):</strong> data stored as magnetised regions on spinning platters; read/write head detects/changes polarity. Cheap per GB, but slower and has moving parts (less durable)</li>\n        <li><strong>Optical (CD/DVD/Blu-ray):</strong> data stored as pits/lands read by a laser. Cheap, good for distribution, but slower and more fragile</li>\n        <li><strong>Solid state (SSD/flash):</strong> data stored electronically in flash memory cells, no moving parts. Fast access, durable, silent, but more expensive per GB and has a finite number of write cycles</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Virtual memory</h3>\n      <p>When RAM is full, the OS uses part of secondary storage as an extension of RAM (\"swap space\"), moving inactive data out of RAM to make room. This allows more programs to run than physical RAM would normally allow, but is much slower than RAM — overuse causes \"thrashing\" (excessive swapping that severely degrades performance).</p>\n      <h3>Buffers</h3>\n      <p>A buffer is temporary storage that compensates for the speed difference between two devices (e.g. a fast CPU and a slower printer), allowing data to be sent in a steady stream without the faster device waiting for the slower one.</p>\n      <h3>Choosing storage for a scenario</h3>\n      <p>Exam questions often ask you to recommend a storage type for a given scenario. Consider: speed needed, capacity needed, cost budget, durability/portability requirements, and whether data needs to be rewritten frequently. E.g. a rugged action camera benefits from SSD/flash storage (durable, no moving parts to be damaged by shock); a cheap mass-produced movie disc benefits from optical storage (cheap to mass-produce, read-only is fine).</p>\n      <h3>Input and output devices</h3>\n      <p>Input devices (e.g. keyboard, mouse, scanner, sensors) convert real-world actions/data into a digital signal the computer can process. Output devices (e.g. monitor, printer, speakers) convert digital data back into a human-perceivable form. Sensors (e.g. temperature, light, pressure) are a special input device type common in embedded/control systems, feeding continuous real-world data to a processor.</p>",
  "Systems Software": "<h3>Functions of an operating system</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Memory management:</strong> allocating memory to processes, using techniques like paging (fixed-size blocks) and segmentation (variable-size logical units)</li>\n        <li><strong>Process scheduling:</strong> deciding which process runs on the CPU and when</li>\n        <li><strong>Peripheral management:</strong> managing input/output devices via device drivers</li>\n        <li><strong>Interrupt handling:</strong> responding to signals requiring immediate CPU attention</li>\n        <li><strong>Security:</strong> user accounts, permissions, access control</li>\n        <li><strong>Providing a user interface</strong> (GUI or CLI)</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Scheduling algorithms</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>First Come First Served (FCFS):</strong> processes run in the order they arrive</li>\n        <li><strong>Round Robin:</strong> each process gets a fixed time slice in turn, cycling through all processes fairly</li>\n        <li><strong>Priority scheduling:</strong> higher-priority processes run first</li>\n        <li><strong>Multilevel feedback queue:</strong> processes move between different priority queues based on behaviour/history</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Interrupts</h3>\n      <p>A signal to the CPU indicating an event needs immediate attention (e.g. a key press, a hardware error). The CPU finishes its current instruction, saves its state, runs the relevant Interrupt Service Routine (ISR), then resumes the original task. This is more efficient than the CPU constantly polling devices to check for events.</p>\n      <h3>Virtual machines</h3>\n      <p>Software that emulates a physical computer, running its own OS. A <strong>Type 1 hypervisor</strong> runs directly on the hardware (bare-metal). A <strong>Type 2 hypervisor</strong> runs on top of a host operating system. Benefits: hardware independence, isolation (for testing/security), running multiple OS instances on one machine.</p>\n      <h3>Worked example: comparing scheduling algorithms</h3>\n      <p>Three processes arrive together needing 4, 1, and 8 seconds of CPU time respectively. Under FCFS in that order, the second (1-second) process must wait 4 seconds before it can even start, despite being very short. Under round robin (e.g. 2-second slices), the short process would complete much sooner since it gets regular turns — this is why round robin feels more \"responsive\" for interactive systems, even though total completion time for all processes may be similar.</p>\n      <h3>Interrupt priorities</h3>\n      <p>Not all interrupts are equally urgent — a hardware failure interrupt typically takes priority over a routine keyboard input interrupt. The OS maintains an interrupt priority system so that if multiple interrupts occur, the most critical is serviced first.</p>",
  "Applications Generation (Translators)": "<h3>Types of translator</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Compiler:</strong> translates the entire source code into machine code BEFORE execution, producing a standalone executable file</li>\n        <li><strong>Interpreter:</strong> translates and executes source code line by line, at runtime, without producing a stored executable</li>\n        <li><strong>Assembler:</strong> translates assembly language (mnemonics) directly into machine code, usually one-to-one</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Compiler vs interpreter trade-offs</h3>\n      <table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;margin:8px 0;\">\n        <tr><th style=\"padding:4px 12px;text-align:left;\"></th><th style=\"padding:4px 12px;text-align:left;\">Compiler</th><th style=\"padding:4px 12px;text-align:left;\">Interpreter</th></tr>\n        <tr><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">Speed</td><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">Faster execution (pre-translated)</td><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">Slower (translated every run)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">Debugging</td><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">Errors reported after full compilation</td><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">Errors reported immediately, line by line</td></tr>\n        <tr><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">Distribution</td><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">Only the executable is needed</td><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">Source code + interpreter needed</td></tr>\n      </table>\n      <h3>Stages of compilation</h3>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Lexical analysis:</strong> breaks source code into tokens (keywords, identifiers, operators, literals), removing whitespace/comments</li>\n        <li><strong>Syntax analysis:</strong> checks tokens against the language's grammar rules, building a parse tree; identifies syntax errors</li>\n        <li><strong>Semantic analysis:</strong> checks for logical consistency (e.g. type checking, variables declared before use)</li>\n        <li><strong>Code generation:</strong> produces the target machine code</li>\n        <li><strong>Code optimisation:</strong> improves the generated code's efficiency (speed/size) without changing its behaviour</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h3>Linkers and loaders</h3>\n      <p>A <strong>linker</strong> combines compiled object code files and library code into a single executable. A <strong>loader</strong> copies that executable into memory and prepares it for execution.</p>\n      <h3>Worked example: lexical vs syntax vs semantic errors</h3>\n      <p>Consider the line of code: <code>x = 5 +</code> (incomplete expression), <code>x === 5</code> used where <code>x = 5</code> was meant (valid syntax, wrong logic), and <code>y = x + \"hello\"</code> where x is an integer (type mismatch). The first is a <strong>syntax error</strong> (incomplete/invalid grammar) caught during syntax analysis. The type mismatch is a <strong>semantic error</strong> caught during semantic analysis — the grammar is valid, but the meaning is inconsistent. A misspelt keyword like <code>PRIN \"hi\"</code> would fail at the <strong>lexical</strong> stage since \"PRIN\" isn't a recognised token.</p>\n      <h3>Two-pass assembly</h3>\n      <p>Assemblers often work in two passes: the first pass builds a symbol table of labels/addresses; the second pass uses that table to fully translate instructions into machine code, resolving any forward references (labels used before they're defined).</p>",
  "Software Development Methodologies": "<h3>Waterfall</h3>\n      <p>A linear, sequential process: requirements → design → implementation → testing → maintenance. Each stage must be fully completed before the next begins.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Advantage:</strong> clear structure, easy to manage and document, works well when requirements are well understood upfront</li>\n        <li><strong>Disadvantage:</strong> inflexible — expensive/difficult to go back and change earlier decisions; testing happens late, so problems are found late</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Agile</h3>\n      <p>Iterative and incremental — software is developed in small chunks (\"sprints\"), with frequent collaboration with the client and flexibility to change requirements throughout.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Advantage:</strong> flexible to changing requirements, working software delivered frequently, early feedback</li>\n        <li><strong>Disadvantage:</strong> less predictable overall timeline/cost, requires close, ongoing client involvement</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Extreme Programming (XP)</h3>\n      <p>An agile approach emphasising technical practices: <strong>pair programming</strong> (two developers at one workstation, one coding and one reviewing), test-driven development, continuous integration, and frequent small releases.</p>\n      <h3>Spiral model</h3>\n      <p>Combines iterative development with a strong focus on risk analysis at each cycle (\"loop\") — suited to large, high-risk projects.</p>\n      <h3>Rapid Application Development (RAD)</h3>\n      <p>Prioritises quick prototyping and user feedback over extensive upfront planning/documentation, aiming for fast delivery.</p>\n      <h3>Choosing a methodology — worked scenario</h3>\n      <p>A bank commissioning a new core transaction-processing system, with strict regulatory sign-off requirements and well-defined, unchanging requirements, would suit <strong>waterfall</strong> — the extensive documentation at each stage supports audit/compliance needs. A startup building a new consumer app, where the exact features desired by users are still being discovered, would suit <strong>agile</strong> — frequent releases let them test ideas with real users and pivot quickly.</p>\n      <h3>Prototyping</h3>\n      <p>Building an early, simplified working version of a system (or part of it) to demonstrate a concept, gather feedback, and clarify requirements before committing to full development. Can be \"throwaway\" (discarded after informing the design) or \"evolutionary\" (refined into the final system).</p>",
  "Compression, Encryption and Hashing": "<h3>Compression</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Lossy:</strong> permanently discards some data to shrink file size significantly (used where minor quality loss is acceptable, e.g. JPEG images, MP3 audio)</li>\n        <li><strong>Lossless:</strong> fully reversible — no data is lost (needed for text, programs, e.g. ZIP)</li>\n        <li><strong>Run Length Encoding (RLE):</strong> replaces consecutive repeated values with a count and the value (e.g. AAAAA → 5A) — effective for data with long runs of repeated values</li>\n        <li><strong>Huffman coding:</strong> assigns shorter binary codes to more frequently occurring symbols (and longer codes to rare ones), based on a frequency-ordered binary tree — reduces overall average code length</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Encryption</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Symmetric encryption:</strong> the same key is used to encrypt and decrypt — fast, but the key must be securely shared between parties</li>\n        <li><strong>Asymmetric encryption:</strong> uses a key pair — a public key (shared openly) to encrypt, and a private key (kept secret) to decrypt. Solves the key-distribution problem since the private key is never transmitted</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Hashing</h3>\n      <p>A hash function converts an input of any size into a fixed-size output (the hash/digest). It is one-way (cannot be reversed to find the original input) and deterministic (same input always gives same output).</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Used to store passwords securely (the actual password is never stored, only its hash)</li>\n        <li>Used to verify data integrity (comparing hashes before/after transfer)</li>\n        <li>Used in hash tables for fast data lookup</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>A <strong>collision</strong> occurs when two different inputs produce the same hash output — a good hash function minimises the likelihood of this.</p>\n      <p class='exam-tip'><strong>Exam tip:</strong> don't confuse encryption (reversible, needs a key) with hashing (irreversible, no key needed to compute) — this is a very common mix-up.</p>\n      <h3>Worked example: Run Length Encoding</h3>\n      <p>The string \"WWWWWWWWWWWWBWWWWWWWWWWWWBBBWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWWBWWWWWWWWWWWWWW\" (a common RLE textbook example describing a scanned black-and-white image row) would be encoded as something like \"12W1B12W3B24W1B14W\" — far shorter than the original, because long runs of the same colour repeat.</p>\n      <h3>Worked example: caesar cipher vs modern encryption</h3>\n      <p>A simple substitution cipher (like the Caesar cipher, shifting each letter by a fixed amount) is easy to break by frequency analysis or simply trying all 25 shifts. Modern symmetric encryption (like AES) uses a vastly larger key space and much more complex mathematical transformations, making brute-force attacks computationally infeasible.</p>\n      <h3>Salting passwords</h3>\n      <p>A \"salt\" is random data added to a password before hashing, so that even identical passwords produce different hashes for different users — this defeats precomputed \"rainbow table\" attacks that rely on matching hashes to a lookup table of common password hashes.</p>",
  "Databases": "<h3>Keys</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Primary key:</strong> a field (or combination of fields) that uniquely identifies each record in a table</li>\n        <li><strong>Foreign key:</strong> a field in one table that references the primary key of another table, creating a relationship between them</li>\n        <li><strong>Composite key:</strong> a primary key made up of two or more fields combined</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Normalisation</h3>\n      <p>The process of organising data to reduce redundancy and prevent update/insertion/deletion anomalies.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>1NF:</strong> no repeating groups; every cell holds a single (atomic) value</li>\n        <li><strong>2NF:</strong> 1NF, plus no partial dependency — every non-key attribute depends on the WHOLE primary key (relevant when there's a composite key)</li>\n        <li><strong>3NF:</strong> 2NF, plus no transitive dependency — every non-key attribute depends ONLY on the primary key, not on another non-key attribute</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>SQL essentials</h3>\n      <pre style=\"background:#1c2129;padding:10px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;\">SELECT * FROM Students WHERE age > 18;\nINSERT INTO Books (title, author) VALUES ('1984', 'Orwell');\nUPDATE Students SET age = 19 WHERE id = 5;\nDELETE FROM Books WHERE id = 3;\nSELECT Students.name, Grades.grade\nFROM Students\nJOIN Grades ON Students.id = Grades.student_id;</pre>\n      <h3>Transactions and ACID</h3>\n      <p>A <strong>transaction</strong> is a sequence of operations treated as a single logical unit of work. <strong>ACID</strong> properties ensure reliability:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Atomicity:</strong> a transaction either fully completes or is fully rolled back — no partial updates</li>\n        <li><strong>Consistency:</strong> a transaction takes the database from one valid state to another</li>\n        <li><strong>Isolation:</strong> concurrent transactions don't interfere with each other</li>\n        <li><strong>Durability:</strong> once committed, changes persist even after a system failure</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Record locking</h3>\n      <p>Prevents two users from editing the same record simultaneously, avoiding the \"lost update\" problem where one user's changes overwrite another's.</p>\n      <h3>Worked example: normalising a table</h3>\n      <p>An unnormalised table storing OrderID, CustomerName, CustomerAddress, Product1, Product2, Product3 has repeating groups (Product1-3) — violates 1NF. Splitting into a separate Orders and OrderItems table (one row per item, linked by OrderID) fixes 1NF. If CustomerAddress depends only on CustomerName (not the whole OrderID+Product key), that's a partial/transitive dependency, fixed by moving customer details into their own Customers table, linked by a CustomerID foreign key — this is the normalisation process toward 3NF in practice.</p>\n      <h3>Entity relationship diagrams</h3>\n      <p>An ERD (Entity Relationship Diagram) visually models the entities (tables) in a database and the relationships between them (one-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-many), used during the design stage before creating the actual tables.</p>",
  "Networks": "<h3>Network types and topologies</h3>\n      <p><strong>LAN</strong> (Local Area Network) covers a small geographic area (e.g. one building); <strong>WAN</strong> (Wide Area Network) spans a large geographic area, connecting multiple LANs (e.g. the internet).</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Star topology:</strong> all devices connect to a central switch/hub. If one cable fails, only that device is affected — but if the central device fails, the whole network goes down</li>\n        <li><strong>Bus topology:</strong> all devices connect to a single central cable (backbone). Cheap and simple, but a break in the backbone can disable the whole network, and performance degrades as more devices are added</li>\n        <li><strong>Mesh topology:</strong> devices are interconnected (fully or partially), providing multiple paths and high resilience, but is expensive and complex to set up</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Client-server vs peer-to-peer</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Client-server:</strong> a dedicated server provides centralised resources/services to client devices; easier to manage security and backups centrally</li>\n        <li><strong>Peer-to-peer:</strong> no central server — each device (peer) can act as both client and server, sharing resources directly; no single point of failure, but harder to manage security/backups consistently</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Protocols and layers</h3>\n      <p>A protocol is an agreed set of rules for communication between devices. The <strong>TCP/IP model</strong> organises network communication into layers, each with a specific responsibility, allowing standardisation and interoperability between different systems/manufacturers.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>TCP (Transmission Control Protocol):</strong> breaks data into packets, ensures reliable, ordered delivery</li>\n        <li><strong>IP (Internet Protocol):</strong> handles addressing and routing of packets between networks</li>\n        <li><strong>HTTP/HTTPS:</strong> protocols for transferring web pages (HTTPS is the encrypted/secure version)</li>\n        <li><strong>FTP:</strong> File Transfer Protocol, for transferring files</li>\n        <li><strong>SMTP:</strong> Simple Mail Transfer Protocol, for sending email</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Network hardware</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Router:</strong> connects different networks together, directing packets based on IP address</li>\n        <li><strong>Switch:</strong> forwards data to the correct device on the SAME network, based on MAC address</li>\n        <li><strong>NIC (Network Interface Card):</strong> hardware allowing a device to connect to a network, each with a unique MAC address</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>MAC vs IP address, and packet switching</h3>\n      <p>A <strong>MAC address</strong> is a fixed, unique physical hardware address burned into a NIC. An <strong>IP address</strong> is a logical address assigned to a device on a network, which can change. <strong>Packet switching</strong> breaks data into packets that are sent independently — potentially via different routes — and reassembled at the destination, allowing efficient sharing of network paths and resilience to congestion/failure.</p>\n      <h3>The layers of the TCP/IP model</h3>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Application layer:</strong> where protocols like HTTP, FTP, SMTP operate — the interface for user-facing applications</li>\n        <li><strong>Transport layer:</strong> TCP breaks data into packets, adds sequencing info, ensures reliable delivery and reassembly</li>\n        <li><strong>Internet layer:</strong> IP handles addressing and routing packets between networks</li>\n        <li><strong>Link layer:</strong> handles the physical transmission of data over the actual network hardware/medium</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h3>Worked example: packet journey</h3>\n      <p>When you request a web page: your browser (application layer, HTTP) hands data to TCP, which splits it into numbered packets (transport layer); IP addresses each packet for routing (internet layer); packets travel across physical links, possibly via several routers, potentially by different paths (link layer); at the destination, TCP reassembles the packets in the correct order using their sequence numbers, even if they arrived out of order.</p>",
  "Web Technologies": "<h3>Core web languages</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>HTML</strong> (HyperText Markup Language): structures content on a web page (headings, paragraphs, links, images)</li>\n        <li><strong>CSS</strong> (Cascading Style Sheets): styles the appearance/presentation of a web page (colours, layout, fonts)</li>\n        <li><strong>JavaScript:</strong> adds interactivity and dynamic behaviour, typically running client-side in the browser</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Client-side vs server-side processing</h3>\n      <p><strong>Client-side</strong> processing happens in the user's browser (e.g. JavaScript form validation) — gives instant feedback and reduces server load, but code is visible/editable by the user and can be bypassed, so it isn't secure alone. <strong>Server-side</strong> processing happens on the web server before the page is sent (e.g. checking a password against a database) — required for anything needing secure/private data access.</p>\n      <h3>Search engine indexing</h3>\n      <p>Search engines use automated programs (\"crawlers\"/\"spiders\") to browse the web, following links to discover pages. Discovered pages are analysed and stored in a searchable index, allowing fast retrieval of relevant results for a search query without searching the entire web in real time. Ranking algorithms (e.g. considering keyword relevance, number/quality of links to a page) determine result order.</p>\n      <h3>Cookies</h3>\n      <p>Small pieces of data stored by a website on the user's browser to remember information between visits (e.g. login sessions, shopping cart contents, preferences). Raises privacy concerns since cookies can be used to track user behaviour across websites for targeted advertising.</p>\n      <h3>PageRank and ranking factors</h3>\n      <p>Beyond simple keyword matching, search engines use algorithms (like Google's original PageRank) that consider the number and quality of other pages linking TO a page as a signal of its importance/trustworthiness — a page linked to by many reputable sites ranks higher than one with few/no incoming links, all else being equal.</p>\n      <h3>Session vs persistent cookies</h3>\n      <p>A <strong>session cookie</strong> is temporary and deleted when the browser closes (e.g. keeping you logged in only for that browsing session). A <strong>persistent cookie</strong> remains stored for a set period, even after the browser closes (e.g. \"remember me\" login, or long-term ad tracking).</p>",
  "Data Types and Number Representation": "<h3>Binary and hexadecimal</h3>\n      <p>Computers store all data as binary (base 2). Hexadecimal (base 16) is used as a more human-readable shorthand for binary — each hex digit represents exactly 4 binary bits.</p>\n      <h3>Two's complement</h3>\n      <p>Used to represent negative integers in binary. To find the two's complement of a positive binary number: invert all bits (one's complement), then add 1. The leftmost (most significant) bit indicates the sign — 1 for negative.</p>\n      <h3>Floating point representation</h3>\n      <p>Represents real (fractional) numbers using a <strong>mantissa</strong> (the significant digits) and an <strong>exponent</strong> (indicates the position of the binary point). <strong>Normalisation</strong> adjusts the mantissa so there is exactly one non-zero digit before the binary point, maximising precision for the available bits. Because only a finite number of bits are available, many real numbers cannot be represented exactly, causing rounding errors.</p>\n      <h3>Binary arithmetic</h3>\n      <p>Binary addition follows the same carrying rules as denary addition (0+0=0, 1+0=1, 1+1=10 carry 1, 1+1+1=11 carry 1). <strong>Overflow</strong> occurs when a calculation produces a result too large to fit within the available number of bits, giving an incorrect result.</p>\n      <h3>Character encoding</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>ASCII:</strong> uses 7 bits (128 characters) to represent English letters, digits, and basic symbols</li>\n        <li><strong>Unicode:</strong> a much larger character set (supporting many languages/symbols worldwide), using variable-length encodings like UTF-8</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Worked example: floating point representation</h3>\n      <p>Consider an 8-bit floating point format with a 5-bit mantissa and 3-bit exponent. The denary value 5.0 (binary 101.0) is normalised to 1.01 × 2², so the mantissa stores \"1010...\" (the leading 1 assumed/implied in some formats) and the exponent stores 2. This shows why precision is limited — only 5 bits are available for the mantissa's significant digits, so numbers requiring more precision than that will be rounded.</p>\n      <h3>Bit patterns for common units</h3>\n      <p>A <strong>bit</strong> is a single binary digit (0 or 1). A <strong>byte</strong> is 8 bits. A <strong>nibble</strong> is 4 bits (exactly one hex digit). Standard multiples: 1 KB = 1024 bytes, 1 MB = 1024 KB, 1 GB = 1024 MB (using the binary/IEC convention often assumed at A-level, though marketing sometimes uses decimal 1000-based multiples).</p>",
  "Data Structures": "<h3>Arrays and records</h3>\n      <p>An <strong>array</strong> holds multiple items of the SAME data type, accessed by index. A <strong>record</strong> groups together related fields that can be of DIFFERENT data types (like a row in a table).</p>\n      <h3>Stacks (LIFO)</h3>\n      <p>Last In, First Out. Supports <strong>push</strong> (add to top) and <strong>pop</strong> (remove from top). Used for: undo functionality, function call management (call stack), checking balanced brackets, depth-first search.</p>\n      <h3>Queues (FIFO)</h3>\n      <p>First In, First Out. Supports <strong>enqueue</strong> (add to back) and <strong>dequeue</strong> (remove from front). Variants: <strong>circular queue</strong> (wraps around to reuse freed space), <strong>priority queue</strong> (items dequeued by priority, not just arrival order). Used for: print queues, CPU task scheduling, breadth-first search.</p>\n      <h3>Trees</h3>\n      <p>A hierarchical structure of nodes. A <strong>binary tree</strong> has each node with at most two children. A <strong>binary search tree (BST)</strong> orders nodes so the left child &lt; parent &lt; right child, enabling efficient searching (similar to binary search, roughly O(log n) for a balanced tree).</p>\n      <h3>Graphs</h3>\n      <p>A collection of nodes (vertices) connected by edges. Can be <strong>directed</strong> (edges have direction) or <strong>undirected</strong>, and <strong>weighted</strong> (edges have an associated cost/value) or unweighted. Used to model networks, maps, relationships.</p>\n      <h3>Hash tables</h3>\n      <p>Uses a hash function to map keys directly to array indices, giving very fast (average O(1)) lookup. A <strong>collision</strong> (two keys hashing to the same index) can be resolved via <strong>chaining</strong> (storing a linked list at that index) or <strong>open addressing</strong> (finding the next free slot).</p>\n      <h3>Worked example: stack trace for balanced brackets</h3>\n      <p>Checking \"( [ ] )\" — push '(' → stack: [(]; push '[' → stack: [(, []; see ']', pop '[' — matches ✓ → stack: [(]; see ')', pop '(' — matches ✓ → stack: []. Stack is empty at the end, so the brackets are balanced. If instead we had \"( [ )\", when we see ')' we'd pop '[' which does NOT match ')' — immediately signalling unbalanced brackets.</p>\n      <h3>Linked lists</h3>\n      <p>A dynamic data structure where each node contains data plus a pointer/reference to the next node. Unlike arrays, a linked list doesn't need contiguous memory and can grow/shrink easily, but doesn't allow direct/instant access to an item by index (must traverse from the start).</p>\n      <h3>Tree traversal orders</h3>\n      <p>For a binary tree: <strong>pre-order</strong> visits root, then left subtree, then right subtree. <strong>In-order</strong> visits left subtree, then root, then right subtree (for a BST, this visits nodes in sorted order). <strong>Post-order</strong> visits left subtree, then right subtree, then root.</p>",
  "Boolean Algebra": "<h3>Logic gates and truth tables</h3>\n      <table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;margin:8px 0;\">\n        <tr><th style=\"padding:4px 12px;text-align:left;\">Gate</th><th style=\"padding:4px 12px;text-align:left;\">Output is 1 when...</th></tr>\n        <tr><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">AND</td><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">Both inputs are 1</td></tr>\n        <tr><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">OR</td><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">At least one input is 1</td></tr>\n        <tr><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">NOT</td><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">Input is 0 (inverts)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">NAND</td><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">NOT both inputs are 1 (inverted AND)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">NOR</td><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">Neither input is 1 (inverted OR)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">XOR</td><td style=\"padding:4px 12px;\">Inputs are different</td></tr>\n      </table>\n      <h3>Boolean algebra laws</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>De Morgan's first law:</strong> NOT(A AND B) = NOT A OR NOT B</li>\n        <li><strong>De Morgan's second law:</strong> NOT(A OR B) = NOT A AND NOT B</li>\n        <li><strong>Distributive law:</strong> A.(B+C) = A.B + A.C</li>\n        <li><strong>Absorption law:</strong> A + A.B = A</li>\n        <li><strong>Double negation:</strong> NOT(NOT A) = A</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Karnaugh maps</h3>\n      <p>A visual grid method for simplifying Boolean expressions by grouping adjacent 1s (in groups of powers of 2), reducing the number of terms/literals needed in the final simplified expression.</p>\n      <h3>Adders</h3>\n      <p>A <strong>half adder</strong> adds two single bits, producing a sum and a carry-out. A <strong>full adder</strong> also accepts a carry-in, allowing three bits to be added — full adders are chained together to build multi-bit binary adders.</p>\n      <h3>Worked example: building a half adder</h3>\n      <p>A half adder can be built from one XOR gate (for the sum: A XOR B) and one AND gate (for the carry: A AND B). Check: A=1,B=1 → sum=XOR(1,1)=0, carry=AND(1,1)=1, giving \"10\" which is correct binary for 1+1=2.</p>\n      <h3>Logic circuits from truth tables</h3>\n      <p>To design a circuit from a truth table, write a Boolean expression using OR of AND terms for every row where the output is 1 (this is called Sum of Products form), then simplify using Boolean algebra or a Karnaugh map before building the circuit — this typically reduces the number of gates needed.</p>",
  "Algorithms: Searching, Sorting & Graph Traversal": "<h3>Searching algorithms</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Linear search:</strong> checks each item in turn from the start. Works on unsorted data. Worst case: O(n)</li>\n        <li><strong>Binary search:</strong> repeatedly halves the search space by comparing the middle item. Requires the data to be SORTED first. Worst case: O(log n)</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Sorting algorithms</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Bubble sort:</strong> repeatedly compares and swaps adjacent out-of-order elements, passing through the list until no swaps are needed. O(n²)</li>\n        <li><strong>Insertion sort:</strong> builds a sorted section of the list one item at a time, inserting each new item into its correct position. O(n²), efficient for nearly-sorted data</li>\n        <li><strong>Merge sort:</strong> divide and conquer — recursively splits the list in half until single elements remain, then merges pairs back together in sorted order. O(n log n)</li>\n        <li><strong>Quicksort:</strong> divide and conquer — picks a pivot, partitions elements smaller/larger than the pivot, then recursively sorts each partition. Average O(n log n), worst case O(n²) if the pivot is consistently poorly chosen</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Big O notation</h3>\n      <p>Describes how an algorithm's time (or space) requirements grow as the input size (n) increases, focusing on the worst case and ignoring constant factors. Common complexities, fastest to slowest growth: O(1) &lt; O(log n) &lt; O(n) &lt; O(n log n) &lt; O(n²) &lt; O(2ⁿ).</p>\n      <h3>Graph traversal</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Breadth-First Search (BFS):</strong> visits all neighbours of the start node first, then their neighbours, level by level — uses a QUEUE. Good for finding the shortest path in an unweighted graph</li>\n        <li><strong>Depth-First Search (DFS):</strong> explores as far as possible down one branch before backtracking — uses a STACK (or recursion)</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Optimisation algorithms</h3>\n      <p><strong>Dijkstra's algorithm</strong> finds the shortest path from a start node to all other nodes in a weighted graph, by progressively selecting the closest unvisited node and updating distances. <strong>A* search</strong> improves on Dijkstra's by using a heuristic estimate of distance to the goal, focusing the search more efficiently toward the target.</p>\n      <h3>Worked example: tracing bubble sort</h3>\n      <p>Sorting [5, 2, 8, 1] with bubble sort: Pass 1: compare 5,2 → swap → [2,5,8,1]; compare 5,8 → no swap; compare 8,1 → swap → [2,5,1,8]. Pass 2: compare 2,5 → no swap; compare 5,1 → swap → [2,1,5,8]; compare 5,8 → no swap. Pass 3: compare 2,1 → swap → [1,2,5,8]; rest no swaps. Pass 4: no swaps at all, so sorting is complete: [1,2,5,8].</p>\n      <h3>Worked example: tracing binary search</h3>\n      <p>Searching for 11 in [2,4,6,8,10,11,13,15,18] (9 items, indices 0-8): middle index 4 = 10; 11&gt;10, so search the right half (indices 5-8); middle index 6 = 13; 11&lt;13, so search the left half (index 5); middle index 5 = 11 — found in 3 comparisons rather than checking every item.</p>\n      <h3>Comparing sorting algorithm choices</h3>\n      <p>Insertion sort is often preferred over bubble sort for nearly-sorted data since it makes fewer comparisons/swaps in that case. Merge sort is preferred when consistent O(n log n) performance is required regardless of the input's initial order (guaranteed, unlike quicksort's worst case), though it needs extra memory. Quicksort is often fastest in practice on average, despite its poor worst case, due to good cache performance and low overhead.</p>",
  "Elements of Computational Thinking": "<h3>Abstraction</h3>\n      <p>Removing unnecessary detail to focus on the essential features relevant to solving a problem. Allows complex real-world systems to be represented in a simplified, manageable way (e.g. a tube map abstracts real geography into simplified lines/stations).</p>\n      <h3>Decomposition</h3>\n      <p>Breaking a complex problem down into smaller, more manageable sub-problems that can be tackled (and often tested) independently, then combined to solve the whole problem.</p>\n      <h3>Algorithmic thinking</h3>\n      <p>Devising a clear, step-by-step, unambiguous sequence of instructions (an algorithm) that solves a problem or class of problems.</p>\n      <h3>Thinking ahead</h3>\n      <p>Anticipating what inputs, outputs, and pre-conditions an algorithm/program will need before writing it, including identifying reusable components and caching opportunities to improve efficiency.</p>\n      <h3>Thinking procedurally</h3>\n      <p>Identifying the required steps to solve a problem and working out the correct order/sequence for them.</p>\n      <h3>Thinking logically</h3>\n      <p>Identifying similarities and patterns between problems, and reasoning carefully about conditions and decisions within an algorithm.</p>\n      <h3>Thinking concurrently</h3>\n      <p>Considering how multiple tasks or processes could be organised to run simultaneously (in parallel), to improve overall efficiency.</p>\n      <h3>Worked example: applying computational thinking to a real problem</h3>\n      <p>Designing a school timetabling system: <strong>decomposition</strong> splits the problem into sub-problems (assign teachers, assign rooms, avoid clashes, respect subject requirements); <strong>abstraction</strong> ignores irrelevant details like teachers' personal preferences for desk position and focuses on the essential constraints (availability, room capacity, subject specialism); <strong>algorithmic thinking</strong> devises a clear method (e.g. assign the most constrained classes first); <strong>thinking ahead</strong> considers what happens when a clash is unavoidable and how the system should respond.</p>\n      <h3>Why computational thinking matters beyond programming</h3>\n      <p>These skills are transferable well beyond writing code — they're used in problem-solving in science, engineering, business, and everyday life, wherever complex problems benefit from being broken down and reasoned about systematically.</p>",
  "Programming Techniques": "<h3>Variables and constants</h3>\n      <p>A <strong>variable</strong>'s value can change during program execution; a <strong>constant</strong>'s value is fixed once set. Use meaningful identifier names for readability.</p>\n      <h3>Selection and iteration</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Selection:</strong> IF/ELSE, CASE/SWITCH — choosing between different paths of execution based on a condition</li>\n        <li><strong>Count-controlled iteration:</strong> FOR loop — repeats a known number of times</li>\n        <li><strong>Condition-controlled iteration:</strong> WHILE loop (checks condition BEFORE each iteration, may run zero times) and DO-WHILE/REPEAT-UNTIL loop (checks condition AFTER, always runs at least once)</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Subroutines</h3>\n      <p>A <strong>function</strong> returns a value; a <strong>procedure</strong> does not (necessarily). Subroutines improve modularity, reusability, and make code easier to test and maintain.</p>\n      <h3>Parameter passing</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>By value:</strong> a COPY of the argument is passed — changes inside the subroutine do NOT affect the original variable</li>\n        <li><strong>By reference:</strong> the actual memory address is passed — changes inside the subroutine DO affect the original variable</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Scope: local vs global variables</h3>\n      <p>A <strong>local</strong> variable is only accessible within the subroutine/block it's declared in. A <strong>global</strong> variable is accessible from anywhere in the program. Overusing global variables risks naming conflicts and unintended side effects, making code harder to debug.</p>\n      <h3>Recursion</h3>\n      <p>A subroutine that calls itself to solve a smaller instance of the same problem. Every recursive routine MUST have a <strong>base case</strong> (a condition that stops further recursive calls) to avoid infinite recursion and a stack overflow.</p>\n      <pre style=\"background:#1c2129;padding:10px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;\">FUNCTION factorial(n)\n  IF n <= 1 THEN\n    RETURN 1\n  ELSE\n    RETURN n * factorial(n - 1)\n  ENDIF\nENDFUNCTION</pre>\n      <h3>Exception handling and IDE features</h3>\n      <p><strong>Exception handling</strong> (try/except or try/catch) lets a program gracefully handle runtime errors without crashing. Useful <strong>IDE</strong> features include syntax highlighting, autocomplete, breakpoints/step-through debugging (pausing execution to inspect variable values), and code formatting tools.</p>\n      <h3>Worked example: tracing recursion</h3>\n      <p>Tracing factorial(4): factorial(4) calls factorial(3), which calls factorial(2), which calls factorial(1) — the base case, returning 1. This unwinds: factorial(2) returns 2×1=2; factorial(3) returns 3×2=6; factorial(4) returns 4×6=24. Each pending call waits on the call stack until the recursive call beneath it returns.</p>\n      <h3>String handling and file handling</h3>\n      <p>Common string operations: concatenation (joining strings), substring extraction, length, case conversion, searching within a string. <strong>File handling</strong> typically involves opening a file (for reading/writing/appending), processing its contents (e.g. line by line), and closing it afterward — always close files to free system resources and ensure data is properly saved.</p>\n      <h3>Common types of runtime error</h3>\n      <p>Division by zero, accessing an array index out of bounds, trying to open a file that doesn't exist, and type mismatches (e.g. trying to add a string to a number without conversion) are all common runtime errors that exception handling can catch and manage gracefully.</p>",
  "Computational Methods": "<h3>Divide and conquer</h3>\n      <p>Breaking a problem into smaller sub-problems of the same type, solving each sub-problem (often recursively), then combining the results. Examples: merge sort, quicksort, binary search.</p>\n      <h3>Backtracking</h3>\n      <p>Trying a possible solution step by step; if a step leads to a dead end (fails), undo (\"backtrack\") the last step and try an alternative. Used for problems like maze-solving, Sudoku, and the N-Queens problem.</p>\n      <h3>Heuristics</h3>\n      <p>A rule-of-thumb / approximate method that finds a good (but not necessarily optimal) solution quickly, trading perfect accuracy for speed. Used when an exact algorithm would take too long to run (e.g. NP-hard problems like the Travelling Salesman Problem).</p>\n      <h3>Data mining</h3>\n      <p>Analysing large datasets to discover previously unknown patterns, trends, and relationships (e.g. identifying customer purchasing patterns from sales data).</p>\n      <h3>Performance modelling</h3>\n      <p>Creating a simulation/model of a system to predict how it will perform under various conditions, before the real system is built — reduces cost/risk.</p>\n      <h3>Pipelining (as a computational method)</h3>\n      <p>Overlapping the stages of processing multiple items so several are processed simultaneously, increasing overall throughput (also seen in CPU architecture, section 1.1).</p>\n      <h3>Visualisation</h3>\n      <p>Representing data or a process graphically (e.g. charts, diagrams, animations) to help humans understand complex information more easily.</p>\n      <h3>Worked example: backtracking through the N-Queens problem</h3>\n      <p>Placing 8 queens on a chessboard so none attack each other: place a queen in the first column's first safe row, move to the next column, place a queen in its first safe row, and so on. If a column has no safe row available given the current placements, backtrack to the previous column and try the next safe row there instead, repeating until a full valid solution is found (or all options are exhausted).</p>\n      <h3>Pipelining as a computational method — worked example</h3>\n      <p>A car factory assembly line processes multiple cars simultaneously at different stages (welding, painting, assembly) rather than fully completing one car before starting the next — this is pipelining applied outside of CPU architecture, and dramatically increases the overall rate of car production.</p>",
  "Programming Paradigms": "<h3>Procedural programming</h3>\n      <p>Code organised as a sequence of instructions grouped into procedures/functions that are called in order. Data and the functions that operate on it are kept separate.</p>\n      <h3>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Class:</strong> a blueprint/template defining the attributes (data) and methods (behaviour) that its objects will have</li>\n        <li><strong>Object:</strong> a specific instance of a class</li>\n        <li><strong>Inheritance:</strong> a subclass can inherit attributes/methods from a superclass, promoting code reuse and establishing an \"is-a\" relationship</li>\n        <li><strong>Encapsulation:</strong> bundling data and the methods that operate on it together, while restricting direct external access to some of an object's internal data (using private/public access modifiers) — protects data integrity</li>\n        <li><strong>Polymorphism:</strong> different classes can respond differently to the same method call/interface (e.g. a shared \"speak\" method behaving differently for different animal subclasses)</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Declarative programming</h3>\n      <p>Programming that states facts and rules describing WHAT the desired outcome is, leaving the underlying system to work out HOW to achieve it — rather than specifying an explicit step-by-step procedure. Examples: Prolog (logic programming), SQL.</p>\n      <h3>Comparing paradigms</h3>\n      <p>OOP models real-world entities naturally and promotes modularity/reuse through classes, making it well suited to large, complex systems. Procedural programming can become harder to manage as a project grows since data and the functions using it are kept separate. Declarative programming is well suited to problems that can be expressed as facts/rules/queries (e.g. database queries) rather than explicit algorithms.</p>\n      <h3>Worked example: a simple class in pseudocode</h3>\n      <pre style=\"background:#1c2129;padding:10px;border-radius:6px;overflow-x:auto;\">CLASS Animal\n  PRIVATE name\n  PUBLIC PROCEDURE NEW(n)\n    name = n\n  ENDPROCEDURE\n  PUBLIC FUNCTION speak()\n    RETURN \"...\"\n  ENDFUNCTION\nENDCLASS\n\nCLASS Dog INHERITS Animal\n  PUBLIC FUNCTION speak()\n    RETURN \"Woof\"\n  ENDFUNCTION\nENDCLASS</pre>\n      <p>Here, Dog inherits from Animal but overrides (\"overloads\") the speak() method — this overriding is what enables polymorphism, since calling speak() on an Animal-type reference actually runs the correct subclass version at runtime.</p>\n      <h3>Method overriding vs overloading</h3>\n      <p><strong>Overriding:</strong> a subclass provides its own specific implementation of a method already defined in its superclass (as above). <strong>Overloading:</strong> multiple methods in the same class share a name but differ in their parameters (e.g. number/type of arguments).</p>",
  "Analysis and Design (Systems Life Cycle)": "<h3>Stages of the systems development life cycle</h3>\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Analysis:</strong> investigating the current system and gathering requirements for the new system (e.g. via interviews, questionnaires, observation)</li>\n        <li><strong>Design:</strong> planning the new system's structure — inputs, outputs, data storage, processing, user interface</li>\n        <li><strong>Implementation:</strong> building/coding the system based on the design</li>\n        <li><strong>Testing:</strong> checking the system works correctly and meets requirements, using a range of test data (normal, boundary, erroneous, extreme)</li>\n        <li><strong>Evaluation:</strong> assessing how well the finished system meets the original requirements</li>\n        <li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> ongoing changes after release — <strong>corrective</strong> (fixing bugs), <strong>adaptive</strong> (updating for a new environment, e.g. new OS/hardware), <strong>perfective</strong> (improving or adding features)</li>\n      </ol>\n      <h3>Design diagrams</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Structure chart:</strong> shows the hierarchical breakdown of a program into modules/subroutines and their calling relationships</li>\n        <li><strong>Flowchart:</strong> shows the sequence of steps/decisions in a process using standard symbols</li>\n        <li><strong>UML (Unified Modeling Language):</strong> a standard notation for visualising system design</li>\n        <ul>\n          <li><strong>Class diagram:</strong> shows classes, their attributes, methods, and relationships between classes</li>\n          <li><strong>Use case diagram:</strong> shows the interactions between users (actors) and the system's functions</li>\n        </ul>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Why iterative testing matters</h3>\n      <p>Testing throughout development (not just at the end) catches errors earlier, when they are cheaper and easier to fix, rather than allowing problems to compound into later, more expensive stages.</p>\n      <h3>Test data types</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Normal data:</strong> valid data that should be accepted (e.g. age = 25)</li>\n        <li><strong>Boundary data:</strong> data right at the edge of what's valid/invalid (e.g. age = 0 or age = 120 for a valid range 0-120)</li>\n        <li><strong>Erroneous data:</strong> invalid data that should be rejected (e.g. age = \"abc\")</li>\n        <li><strong>Extreme data:</strong> the largest/smallest valid values the system is designed to handle</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Worked example: choosing test data</h3>\n      <p>Testing an input field that only accepts ages 0-120: normal test = 30; boundary tests = 0 and 120 (should be accepted), -1 and 121 (should be rejected); erroneous test = \"twenty\" (non-numeric, should be rejected with an appropriate error message).</p>",
  "Legal, Moral, Cultural and Ethical Issues": "<h3>Computer Misuse Act (1990)</h3>\n      <p>Makes it a criminal offence to: gain unauthorised access to computer material (hacking); gain unauthorised access with intent to commit further offences; make unauthorised modifications to computer material (e.g. spreading malware); make, supply, or obtain tools for committing these offences. Applies regardless of whether any actual damage is caused.</p>\n      <h3>Data Protection Act / UK GDPR</h3>\n      <p>Regulates how personal data must be collected, stored and processed. Gives individuals rights including: the right to access data held about them, the right to have inaccurate data corrected, the right to request erasure (\"right to be forgotten\"), and requires data to be kept secure, accurate, and only for as long as necessary.</p>\n      <h3>Copyright, Designs and Patents Act</h3>\n      <p>Protects original work (including software, music, and written content) from being copied, distributed, or used without the creator's permission.</p>\n      <h3>Open source vs proprietary software</h3>\n      <p><strong>Open source:</strong> source code is publicly available and can be freely modified/redistributed (though often under specific licence conditions). <strong>Proprietary:</strong> source code is closed, and usage is restricted by licence terms, typically requiring payment.</p>\n      <h3>Ethical issues in computing</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Algorithmic bias:</strong> AI/algorithms can reflect and amplify biases present in their training data, leading to unfair or discriminatory outcomes (e.g. in recruitment or facial recognition)</li>\n        <li><strong>Automation and employment:</strong> increased automation can displace jobs in roles that become automated, requiring workforce retraining/adaptation</li>\n        <li><strong>Surveillance and privacy:</strong> growing use of data collection and tracking raises concerns about individual privacy</li>\n        <li><strong>Transparency:</strong> complex AI decision-making can be difficult to explain, raising concerns when used for significant decisions (e.g. loan approvals)</li>\n      </ul>\n      <h3>Environmental and social impact</h3>\n      <p>E-waste from discarded devices and the high energy consumption of data centres are significant environmental concerns. The <strong>digital divide</strong> refers to the gap between those who have easy access to computing technology/the internet and those who do not, caused by factors such as income, geographic location (e.g. rural broadband availability), and age.</p>\n      <h3>Worked example: applying multiple laws to one scenario</h3>\n      <p>A company employee copies a customer database (containing names, addresses, and purchase history) onto a personal USB drive without authorisation, intending to sell it to a competitor. This breaches: the <strong>Computer Misuse Act</strong> (unauthorised access/copying of data with intent to commit further offences); the <strong>Data Protection Act/GDPR</strong> (processing/sharing personal data without a lawful basis or consent); and potentially the <strong>Copyright, Designs and Patents Act</strong> if the database structure itself is considered the company's protected intellectual property.</p>\n      <h3>Cultural issues in computing</h3>\n      <p>Software and interfaces designed without considering cultural differences (language, symbols, colour meanings, date formats) can be confusing, offensive, or unusable for users from different backgrounds — good design considers internationalisation and localisation to make systems genuinely accessible worldwide.</p>",
}
